# value_place_once.tkr — the COL-F0a VALUE-regime fixture (crumb 0021). `main.tks` `place`s N
# large `big::Big` value-structs, one per slot, reading each back by copy (`teko::mem::read`) to
# confirm the value landed, and records each stable pointer as an address in a fixed `[]i64` index.
# It then FORCES a grow: a new, larger index of_len'd and the pointers copied across. The values
# are never recopied — every address in the grown index equals the original (the grow moved
# pointers, not values), which is what closes the v1 value-copy leak. Exits 0 only if exactly N
# places happened, the read-back checksum holds, and every pointer survived the grow unchanged.
# The compiler never `place`s a large VALUE-struct itself, so this path is not fixpoint-exercised.
# Pinned to the C backend: the VALUE regime emits on the C route and the native leg honest-stops.

Feature: value_place_once — place writes a large value once; a grow copies pointers, never values

  Scenario: place_once_then_grow_index_copies_pointers_and_never_recopies_the_values
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
